            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>During the walkthrough, we notice air leaks from the following house components:

Doors (Garage and solarium)

Crawl space access hatch

Floor grilles (under the kitchen sink)

The combustion air supply in crawl space

The infiltration test result has shown few leakages in your house. The most important is coming from the combustion air supply for the furnace. Unfortunately, there is no solution, unless you want to replace the heating system by a sealed unit to an electric system. Concerning the doors, the replacement of the weather stripping might help to increase the airtightness of the doors.

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>At the moment of the assessment your attic insulation was R-40. The actual standard for the city of Whitehorse is R-60. There is space to add more insulation. Increasing the insulation in your attic will increase the efficiency of the system to keep the heat inside the house in wintertime and protect you against the heat coming from the attic in your house in the summertime. Also, we recommend adding insulation on your gable wall. This will represent an open door for air leaks. </ceilings>
                <foundation>Part of the radon remediation we recommend the addition of insulation on the crawl space floor. At the moment of the assessment, the floor was not insulated but covered with a vapor barrier. The addition of insulation will decrease cold and humidity to migrate into the living space. </foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>The natural ventilation of your house is not enough to ensure proper ventilation in the house. This situation might lead to an increase in humidity in your home and create condensation on your windows. </VentilationSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>At the moment of the audit, the homeowner mentions the idea of making the solarium part of the house. On the day of the assessment, the solarium was not heated and was not included in the heated space of the house. This space can easily be added to the rest of the house by adding a heating system. Electrical baseboards can be a good solution. </AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
